From: Josh Heinrichs <josh.heinrichs@shopify.com>
Subject: [PATCH] futils: don't rmdir() empty parents through a trailing slash

`git_futils_rmdir_r()` with `GIT_RMDIR_EMPTY_PARENTS` walks up the removed
path's parents with `git_fs_path_walk_up()`, which yields each directory
with a trailing slash, and hands them to rmdir() as they are.

With the trailing slash, a symbolic link in that position is resolved on
BSD-derived systems (including macOS), so rmdir() acts on the link's
target: it removes an unrelated empty directory the link points to, or,
for a link to an ancestor, fails with EINVAL (macOS 27) or ENOTEMPTY
(earlier macOS). Linux reports ENOTDIR for the link itself, which is what
the error handling in `futils__rmdir_empty_parent()` expects and treats as
"nothing to remove".

This is reached from checkout on a case-insensitive filesystem whenever a
file is removed from a directory whose name collides with a symlink, e.g.
`checkout::icase::overwrites_links_for_folders_when_forced`, which fails on
macOS 27 with:

    invalid path for filesystem '.../testrepo/a/': Invalid argument

Strip the trailing slash before calling rmdir(), so the link itself is
named and every platform reports ENOTDIR.
---
--- a/src/util/futils.c	1969-12-31 18:00:01
+++ b/src/util/futils.c	2026-10-09 17:36:44
@@ -829,26 +829,44 @@
 static int futils__rmdir_empty_parent(void *opaque, const char *path)
 {
 	futils__rmdir_data *data = opaque;
+	git_str dir = GIT_STR_INIT;
+	size_t len = strlen(path);
 	int error = 0;
 
-	if (strlen(path) <= data->baselen)
-		error = GIT_ITEROVER;
+	if (len <= data->baselen)
+		return GIT_ITEROVER;
 
-	else if (p_rmdir(path) < 0) {
+	/*
+	 * `git_fs_path_walk_up` yields directories with a trailing slash.
+	 * Strip it before calling rmdir(): with the slash, a symbolic link
+	 * in that position is resolved on BSD-derived systems (including
+	 * macOS), so rmdir() would act on the link's target -- removing an
+	 * unrelated empty directory, or failing with EINVAL for a link to
+	 * an ancestor -- instead of reporting ENOTDIR for the link itself,
+	 * as Linux does and as the error handling below expects.
+	 */
+	while (len > 1 && path[len - 1] == '/')
+		len--;
+
+	if (git_str_put(&dir, path, len) < 0)
+		return -1;
+
+	if (p_rmdir(dir.ptr) < 0) {
 		int en = errno;
 
 		if (en == ENOENT || en == ENOTDIR) {
 			/* do nothing */
 		} else if ((data->flags & GIT_RMDIR_SKIP_NONEMPTY) == 0 &&
 			en == EBUSY) {
-			error = git_fs_path_set_error(errno, path, "rmdir");
+			error = git_fs_path_set_error(en, dir.ptr, "rmdir");
 		} else if (en == ENOTEMPTY || en == EEXIST || en == EBUSY) {
 			error = GIT_ITEROVER;
 		} else {
-			error = git_fs_path_set_error(errno, path, "rmdir");
+			error = git_fs_path_set_error(en, dir.ptr, "rmdir");
 		}
 	}
 
+	git_str_dispose(&dir);
 	return error;
 }
 
